{"language": "en", "duration": 95.16, "text": "You earn more than you did five years ago. So why has your bank account barely moved? Take an example. You earn $2,000 a month. You spend 1900 and you save 100. Your salary goes up to $3,000. A year later, you spend 2,900 and you're still saving 100. This is called lifestyle inflation. Often, every raise becomes the new normal and spending rises to meet it. Behind it is a psychological mechanism, hedonic adaptation. Our brain gets used to improvements fast. The bigger apartment, the Friday dinner out, the more comfortable car. After a few months, it's no longer a luxury. It's the minimum. Psychologists call it the hedonic treadmill. You keep running, but you stay in the same place. And the trap is that each upgrade looks reasonable. A slightly pricier rent, one more subscription, one more trip. No single expense is crazy, but together they swallow the entire raise. So how do you get out of it? First, look at your savings rate, not your salary. What matters isn't what you earn, it's what you keep. Then apply a simple rule to every raise. Half goes to savings or investing before your lifestyle adjusts. You can enjoy the other half without guilt. Finally, choose your upgrades. One or two that truly make you happier. Not everyone your new salary allows. Earning more doesn't make you rich. Keeping more does. And you, which expense has appeared since your last raise? Tell us in the comments.", "words": [{"word": "You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.52}, {"word": "earn", "start": 0.52, "end": 0.9}, {"word": "more", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.24}, {"word": "than", "start": 1.24, "end": 1.48}, {"word": "you", "start": 1.48, "end": 1.6}, {"word": "did", "start": 1.6, "end": 1.8}, {"word": "five", "start": 1.8, "end": 2.2}, {"word": "years", "start": 2.2, "end": 2.5}, {"word": "ago.", "start": 2.5, "end": 2.82}, {"word": "So", "start": 3.04, "end": 3.4}, {"word": "why", "start": 3.4, "end": 3.86}, {"word": "has", "start": 3.86, "end": 4.08},